    "raw_text": "Senator Pisciottano Announces $5.3 Million for Victim Services in Allegheny County\nSeptember 9, 2026\nAllegheny County, PA\n– State\nSen. Nick Pisciottano\n(D-Allegheny) announced that the\nPennsylvania Commission on Crime and Delinquency\n(PCCD), chaired by\nLieutenant Governor Austin Davis\n, has approved $5,333,957 in grant funding to support victims, strengthen public safety, and prevent violence.\nThe funding, awarded through the federal Victims of Crime Act (VOCA), will support organizations providing counseling, shelter, advocacy, legal assistance, and other services to victims and survivors.\n“I’m grateful for the work these organizations do across our region and happy to see these federal resources coming to Allegheny County,” said Senator Pisciottano.\nThe following organizations serving Allegheny County were awarded funding:\nCenter for Victims\n– $1,219,231 in VOCA Funds\nWomen’s Center & Shelter of Greater Pittsburgh\n– $1,114,319 in VOCA Funds\nPittsburgh Action Against Rape\n– $956,074 in VOCA Funds for sexual assault and trafficked victims\nNeighborhood Legal Services Association\n– $567,502 in VOCA Funds for Legal Aid for domestic violence survivors and crime victims\nAlle-Kiski Area Hope Center\n– $469,167 in VOCA Funds\nCrisis Center North\n– $452,307 in VOCA Funds for core and mobile counseling services\nAllegheny County Chief Executive Officer\n– $201,186 in VOCA Funds\nAllegheny County Chief Executive Officer\n– $194,297 in State Act 80 Funds for quality, training, and efficiency enhancements\nA Child’s Place PA\n– $154,571 in VOCA Funds\nCASA of Allegheny County\n– $5,303 in VOCA Funds for training of CASA volunteers\nMore information about the Pennsylvania Commission on Crime and Delinquency and the grants it awards can be found\nhere\n.",
    "ai_summary": {
      "summary": "The Pennsylvania Commission on Crime and Delinquency approved $5.3 million in federal and state grant funding to ten organizations in Allegheny County that provide services to crime victims, including counseling, shelter, legal aid, and sexual assault support. Senator Pisciottano issued a press release announcing the funding. The senator did not secure or vote on this funding; it was approved by the PCCD, a separate state agency.",
